From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 03:36:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10998/11171] Record a false-positive quarantine: markers
 counted from the reduced data

exp6967 is stamped flagged_adversarial with a live CRITICAL DURATION_TOO_SHORT
at 1.86s. Its substrate is deterministic_z3_and_bounded_enumeration_reducer and
1.86s is plausible for that.

The markers driving the flag are 568 gguf, 280 Qwen and 629 gemma matches, and
every one sampled is an attempt_key inside cluster_example_rows -- identifiers
of the upstream attempts this experiment clusters, the data it reduces rather
than a claim that it ran a model. The only cuda hit is in the linter's own flag
message.

So the marker scan treats model names in an artifact's INPUT DATA as evidence
the artifact ran a model, and any reducer over LLM attempt records inherits the
60-second live-inference floor from the records it summarises, then gets
quarantined for finishing quickly.

That is not cosmetic. Capstone and headline-aggregation tasks must skip
flagged_adversarial artifacts, so a false positive removes a real result from
every downstream aggregation with nobody re-reading it.

It also mirrors an audit finding already open: check_duration_vs_claim fails the
other way, returning at the marker guard before its own duration check. Both are
the marker scan carrying more than it can.

Not fixed, and explicitly not by allowlist -- adding the substrate name is the
move this project forbids, silencing one name while leaving every other reducer
exposed. The real fix scopes the marker scan away from declared input data,
which changes what the corpus is judged against.

Today's three such flags sit inside the 3/4/9/1/3 daily base rate, and exp6966
declaring live CUDA at 37.99s is correctly not flagged, so this is a standing
weakness surfacing rather than a regression.

